<h2>Common Claim Issues and Insurer Arguments</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Long-term leak.&rdquo;</strong> The insurer points to staining, rot, or mold as evidence that water leaked over time.</li>
<li><strong>&ldquo;It&rsquo;s flood.&rdquo;</strong> Water at floor level after heavy rain is classified as surface water.</li>
<li><strong>&ldquo;No storm opening.&rdquo;</strong> Interior water after a storm is attributed to an old roof rather than storm damage. See <a href="/services/storm-damage-claims/">storm damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Mitigation disputes.</strong> Drying invoices are challenged as excessive, or the insurer says delayed drying caused avoidable damage.</li>
<li><strong>Mold sublimits.</strong> Mold remediation is capped or excluded.</li>
<li><strong>Matching and continuity.</strong> Only wet sections of flooring or cabinetry are replaced, leaving mismatched finishes.</li>
</ul>

