<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deductible.</strong> Plumbing losses usually fall under the all-other-perils deductible; storm-related roof leaks may fall under the wind/hail deductible.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV.</strong> Replacement cost policies typically pay ACV first, with recoverable depreciation after repairs. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">RCV vs. ACV in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Limits.</strong> Mold, water backup, and tear-out coverage may carry separate caps.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> Replacing plumbing or electrical systems damaged by water must meet McAllen&rsquo;s 2024 ICC codes, effective January 1, 2026. Upgrade costs depend on ordinance or law coverage. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Freeze Damage and the February 2021 Storm</h2>
<p>The February 2021 freeze was unusual for the Rio Grande Valley. Across southern Hidalgo County, lows reached the lower 20s to upper teens, and freezing temperatures lasted for long stretches over several nights. Plumbing in exterior walls, attics, and uninsulated outdoor runs is common in local homes, and many pipes split when water inside them froze. Damage often did not show up until the thaw, when water began flowing again.</p>
<p>Freeze claims can raise specific questions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reasonable care.</strong> Some policies exclude freezing damage unless you maintained heat or shut off and drained the water, especially in vacant or unoccupied buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Date of loss.</strong> The pipe may have split during the freeze but leaked only after the thaw. Both dates should be documented.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden damage.</strong> Leaks inside walls and slabs may not be visible for weeks, leading to later arguments about long-term leakage.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you had a freeze claim that was underpaid or closed before all the damage surfaced, it may be worth a review. Our article on <a href="/freeze-damage-burst-pipe-claims-rio-grande-valley/">freeze damage and burst pipe claims in the RGV</a> covers this in more depth, and our <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claims</a> page explains next steps.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A public adjuster can help identify and document the source of the water, review mitigation records, prepare a complete repair estimate, organize contents, and present the claim. If the insurer has denied the claim as long-term leakage or flood, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>; if payment is stalled, see <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a>. We cannot guarantee an outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Shut off the water and report the loss promptly.</li>
<li>Start mitigation quickly: extraction, drying, and removal of wet materials to limit mold.</li>
<li>Keep the failed part and photograph everything before removal.</li>
<li>Submit a proof of loss when requested, within the policy deadline.</li>
<li>Cooperate with inspections and document requests.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations and Exclusions</h2>
<p>Water claims are frequently limited by exclusions for flood and surface water, continuous or repeated seepage or leakage, wear and tear, deterioration, and faulty plumbing installation; freezing exclusions when heat was not maintained in a vacant or unoccupied building; mold limitations; and water backup exclusions unless an endorsement was purchased. Policies may also set deadlines to file suit. For legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
